  "text": "For any value of \\( n \\) (from \\(+1\\) to \\(n\\)), the expression \\((\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)) \\cdots (\\sin^2(2^n) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1})) \\) results in \\(\\frac{1}{2}.\\) The approach utilized is that \\((\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x))\\) is equivalent to \\(1.\\) \n\nThen, the term \\(\\sin^2(2^n) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1})\\) is associated to mean that \\(\\sin(x)\\) from \\(\\sin(2^n)\\) is tied to the opposite side of the triangle which means \\(\\sin(2^{n+1})\\) from the same triangle.\n\nHence, the product \\((\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)) \\cdots (\\sin^2(2^n) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1})) = \\frac{1}{2}.\\)\n\nThus, the smallest \\(n\\) satisfying the condition is \\(\\boxed{1}.\\)",
